/-!
# Principalization at finitely many regular surface points

Source: Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Tag 0AHH,
`resolve-lemma-make-ideal-principal`, including the effective Cartier conclusion
in the final paragraph of its proof
(blueprint: lem-sfb-surface-point-ideal-principalization).

The support of `I : X.IdealSheafData` is the zero locus of the ideal, hence the
support of the quotient structure sheaf. An ordinary blowup tower retains every
intermediate scheme, its centre and the composite to the original scheme.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A quasi-coherent ideal whose quotient is supported at finitely many closed regular
surface points becomes effective Cartier and an invertible module after finitely many
blowups of reduced closed points over that set. The ordinary tower allows length zero.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0AHH, `resolve-lemma-make-ideal-principal`
(blueprint: lem-sfb-surface-point-ideal-principalization).

Proof plan (the nine steps of the DAG node):
1. For each $p\in T$, put $\ell_p=\operatorname{length}_{\mathcal O_{X,p}}(\mathcal
   O_{X,p}/\mathcal I_p)$. The quotient is cyclic and its support in the spectrum of the stalk is
   contained in the maximal ideal: its other points correspond to proper generizations of $p$,
   which cannot belong to the finite set of closed points $T$. The finite-length criterion [lem-
   sc-generic-stalk-finite-length] applies when this quotient is nonzero; a zero quotient has
   length zero. Thus $N=\sum_{p\in T}\ell_p$ is a natural number. Prove the assertion
   simultaneously for all the stated triples $(X,T,\mathcal I)$ by strong induction on $N$.
   Uses: lem-sc-generic-stalk-finite-length, def-weil-pbc-module-length.
2. If $N=0$, every quotient stalk at $T$ is zero (a nonzero module has the strict submodule chain
   $0\subsetneq M$). Every stalk outside $T$ is zero by hypothesis. Hence $\mathcal I=\mathcal
   O_X$. Its generator $1$ is a nonzerodivisor, so the length-zero sequence satisfies the
   effective Cartier and invertibility conclusions.
   Uses: def-weil-pbc-module-length, def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal, def-sc-invertible-module.
3. If $N>0$, choose $p\in T$ with $\ell_p>0$ and blow up its reduced closed subscheme, writing
   $b:X'\to X$. On a Noetherian affine cover, the centre ideal is finitely generated by [def-weil-
   pbc-noetherian-ring]; the associated ideal sheaf is therefore of finite type by [pqf-def-
   coherent]. By [lemma-nagata-blowup-proper] the morphism is proper, hence of finite type, so
   [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian] makes $X'$ Noetherian. By [thm-nagata-blowup-
   principalizes-center], the extended centre ideal $\mathcal L\subseteq\mathcal O_{X'}$ is
   effective Cartier. It cuts out the scheme-theoretic fibre $E=b^{-1}(p)$, and [thm-nagata-
   global-blowup-complement] identifies $X'\setminus E$ with $X\setminus\{p\}$.
   Uses: lemma-nagata-blowup-proper, def-weil-proper-morphism, lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-
   noetherian, thm-nagata-blowup-principalizes-center, thm-nagata-global-blowup-complement, def-
   weil-pbc-noetherian-ring, pqf-def-coherent.
4. Put $A=\mathcal O_{X,p}$ and $I=\mathcal I_p$. The morphism $\operatorname{Spec}A\to X$ is flat
   by [lem-sbc-canonical-stalk-spectrum-flat]. Use the comparison for this flat map and the
   reduced-point ideal in [def-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change-map]. Its invertibility [thm-nagata-
   blowup-flat-base-change] therefore identifies $Y=X'\times_X\operatorname{Spec}A$ with
   $\operatorname{Bl}_{\mathfrak m_A}(\operatorname{Spec}A)$; the pulled-back reduced centre is
   cut out by $\mathfrak m_A$. The exceptional fibre $E_Y$ is identified with $E$, since both are
   the base change to $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa(p)$. Apply [lem-sfb-local-surface-maximal-blowup-
   geometry] to $A$: $E_Y$ is a projective line, and its closed points have regular local rings of
   dimension $2$ on $Y$.
   Uses: lem-sbc-canonical-stalk-spectrum-flat, thm-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change, lem-sfb-local-
   surface-maximal-blowup-geometry, def-nagata-blowup-flat-base-change-map.
5. The ideal $I$ is proper because $\ell_p>0$, and its quotient has finite length by step 1. Apply
   [lem-sfb-local-surface-weak-transform-colength-drop]. It gives $d\geq1$, $I\mathcal
   O_Y\subseteq\mathcal O_Y(-dE_Y)$ and the residual ideal $\mathcal J_Y=I\mathcal O_Y(dE_Y)$. Its
   quotient is supported on finitely many closed points $S\subset E_Y$, with total local length
   strictly less than $\ell_p$.
   Uses: lem-sfb-local-surface-weak-transform-colength-drop.
6. Here is the global ideal inclusion omitted in the source. On affine rings $R\to B$, the inverse
   image of a closed subscheme $\operatorname{Spec}(R/K)$ has coordinate ring $B/KB$ (the quotient
   universal property). Thus the kernel definition of extended ideal is precisely $KB$. Choose an
   affine neighbourhood $\operatorname{Spec}R$ of $p$, with prime $\mathfrak m$, and an affine
   open $\operatorname{Spec}B\subset b^{-1}(\operatorname{Spec}R)$. For a point $q\in E$ in this
   open, with prime $Q\subset B$, one has $Q\cap R=\mathfrak m$. Set $W=R\setminus\mathfrak m$.
   The corresponding affine base change is $\operatorname{Spec}(W^{-1}B)$, and
   $B_Q\cong(W^{-1}B)_{W^{-1}Q}$: every element of $W$ is already a unit in $B_Q$, and both maps
   send a fraction to the same fraction. The extended ideals of $\mathcal I$ and of the centre
   agree under this identification. Thus the local inclusion of step 5 gives $\mathcal I\mathcal
   O_{X',q}\subseteq\mathcal L_q^d$ at every $q\in E$. Off $E$ the right side is the unit ideal.
   Stalkwise inclusion gives $\mathcal I\mathcal O_{X'}\subseteq\mathcal L^d$. Locally choose a
   nonzerodivisor generator $e$ for $\mathcal L$ and set $\mathcal J=e^{-d}(\mathcal I\mathcal
   O_{X'})\subseteq\mathcal O_{X'}$. Changing $e$ by a unit changes this ideal by that unit's
   inverse $d$th power, so the local ideals agree and glue. They are quasi-coherent, and $\mathcal
   I\mathcal O_{X'}=\mathcal L^d\mathcal J$.
   Uses: def-nagata-extended-ideal, def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal.
7. The stalk identifications of step 6 identify $\mathcal J$ on $E$ with $\mathcal J_Y$, and
   preserve the lengths of its stalk quotients. Off $E$ the factor $\mathcal L$ is the unit ideal,
   so $\mathcal J$ agrees with $\mathcal I$ under $X'\setminus E\cong X\setminus\{p\}$. Let $T'$
   consist of the points corresponding to $S$ and the unique lifts of $T\setminus\{p\}$. These are
   closed in $X'$: the first set is closed in the closed fibre $E$, and each of the other points
   is the entire fibre over its closed image. Their local rings are regular of dimension $2$, by
   steps 4 and 6 on $E$ and the complement isomorphism elsewhere. The quotient $\mathcal
   O_{X'}/\mathcal J$ is supported in $T'$, and $$\sum_{q\in T'}\operatorname{length}_{\mathcal
   O_{X',q}}(\mathcal O_{X',q}/\mathcal J_q)<\ell_p+\sum_{t\in T\setminus\{p\}}\ell_t=N.$$ This
   uses the unweighted local-length inequality of step 5, so no equality of residue fields is
   assumed.
8. Apply the strong induction hypothesis to $(X',T',\mathcal J)$, which satisfies every hypothesis
   by steps 3 and 7 and has strictly smaller invariant. It gives a finite tower $h:Z\to X'$ of
   blowups at reduced closed points over $T'$ such that $\mathcal J\mathcal O_Z$ is effective
   Cartier. Every point of $T'$ maps into $T$. Prepending $b$ therefore gives a finite tower whose
   centres all map into the original $T$.
9. Apply [lemma-nagata-cartier-total-transform-blowup] successively along the finite tower $h$ to
   the effective Cartier ideal $\mathcal L$. Its extension $\mathcal L\mathcal O_Z$ remains
   effective Cartier. Extension of the identity in step 6 yields $$\mathcal I\mathcal
   O_Z=(\mathcal L\mathcal O_Z)^d(\mathcal J\mathcal O_Z).$$ Locally the right side is generated
   by $a^dc$, where $a$ and $c$ are nonzerodivisors. If $a^dcx=0$, repeated cancellation gives
   $x=0$. Thus $\mathcal I\mathcal O_Z$ is effective Cartier; multiplication by $a^dc$ identifies
   its local module with $\mathcal O_Z$, so it is invertible. This closes the induction.
   Uses: lemma-nagata-cartier-total-transform-blowup, def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal, def-sc-
   invertible-module, def-nagata-extended-ideal.
-/
theorem exists_surface_point_ideal_principalization
    (X : Scheme.{u}) [IsNoetherian X]
    (T : Set X) (hT : T.Finite)
    (hclosed : ∀ p ∈ T, IsClosed ({p} : Set X))
    (hregular : ∀ p ∈ T, IsRegularLocalRing (X.presheaf.stalk p))
    (hdim : ∀ p ∈ T, ringKrullDim (X.presheaf.stalk p) = 2)
    (I : X.IdealSheafData) (hsupport : (I.support : Set X) ⊆ T) :
    ∃ A : OrdinaryBlowupTower X,
      (∀ i : Fin A.length,
        ∃ (p : A.level i.castSucc) (hp : IsClosed ({p} : Set (A.level i.castSucc))),
          A.toBase i.castSucc p ∈ T ∧
          A.center i = Scheme.IdealSheafData.vanishingIdeal ⟨{p}, hp⟩) ∧
      (I.comap (A.toBase (Fin.last A.length))).IsInvertible ∧
      Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible (I.comap (A.toBase (Fin.last A.length))).toModules := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
